{"name":"Mirabella","hex":"#886793","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Mirabella","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname from Italian."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Mirabella"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mirabella","blocks":[{"text":"Mirabella was a women's magazine published from June 1989 to April 2000. It was created by and named for Grace Mirabella, a former Vogue editor in chief, in partnership with Rupert Murdoch."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b8/Mirabella_Jan_1997.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirabella","wikidata":"Q6872298"},{"title":"Mirabella (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Mirabella is a 2014 Philippine television drama series broadcast by ABS-CBN. Directed by Erick C. Salud, Jerome C. Pobocan, Jojo A. Saguin and Claudio \"Tots\" Sanchez-Mariscal IV, it stars Julia Barretto, Enrique Gil, Sam Concepcion and Mika dela Cruz. It aired on the network's Primetime Bida line up and worldwide on TFC from March 24 to July 4, 2014, replacing Annaliza, and was replaced by Pure Love."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a1/Mirabellatitlecard.jpg/330px-Mirabellatitlecard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirabella_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q15858704"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Julia_Barretto_2013_Candy_Style_Awards.jpg/500px-Julia_Barretto_2013_Candy_Style_Awards.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Julia_Barretto_2013_Candy_Style_Awards.jpg/1280px-Julia_Barretto_2013_Candy_Style_Awards.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Julia_Barretto_2013_Cand